these wide swamps near Charleston South  Carolina were long a blight on the  landscape and a breeding ground for the  malaria carrying mosquitoes the  Anopheles Quadra maculatus here water  and vegetation met and malaria drained  the vitality of the people living nearby  here the malaria danger signal flash  during hot summer months and the land  was worse than useless  week after week inspectors waded through  these swamps dipping for larvae  the larvae were collected in files to be  transported to a laboratory for  examination larvae sighting was  necessary when the count was high and it  was usually high  these repetitive measures were a  continual drain on public health funds  and public health officials became  convinced but permanent elimination of  the problem by drainage was necessary  and economically feasible malaria  control engineers located the ditches  and established grades  labor crews following the engineers dug  the main ditch from the outlet to the  swamp the initial cut was vertical sides  of the ditch were sloped to a grade  determined by the stability of the soil  and the bottom was fine graded to ensure  a smooth flow of water  excavated material was spread evenly  along the banks so that water could not  collect behind it and to keep the spoil  from washing back into the ditch the  main ditch was put into service by  making an entrance cut or tapping the  pond good engineering and proper  construction paid off as the water moves  smoothly and rapidly out of the swamp  the completed main ditch with its smooth  well sloped sides and spoil spread  evenly along the banks is a good example  of hand ditching smaller areas were  drained into the main ditch by laterals  or side ditches these were also dug from  the outlet to the swamp the pond was  tapped and the water drained off in a  steady flow  to enter the main ditch at angles  designed to avoid any disturbance of  flow in either ditch abrupt angles were  avoided and the discharge enters the  main ditch a few inches above its bottom  complete drainage of this area has made  reproduction of the malaria mosquito  impossible this is no longer a problem  area the land has been reclaimed and its  development may offset the entire cost  of the project here hand itching was the  practical answer to malaria control
 

